      "textEn": "415\n\nShantanurdasakanyayam jagrhe chitrangadah suta. 20. Meaning:\nVir-youth-leader: The best of all warriors, Bhishma; Yena-from whom: The incarnation of Lord Parashurama also; Yudhi-in war; Toshitah-being satisfied (by Bhishma's defeat); Shantanu-from Shantanu; Das-from a Shudra's daughter, Satyavati's womb; Jajne-birthed; Chitrangada-Chitrangada; Sut-son I.\n\nBhishma was the best of all warriors. When he defeated Lord Parashurama in battle,\nParashurama was extremely pleased with him. From the semen of Shantanu, from the womb of the virtuous daughter Satyavati, Chitrangada\nwas born. Meaning: Satyavati was actually the daughter of Uparichara Vasu, who was born from the womb of a fisherwoman named Matsyagarbha. Satyavati was later raised by a fisherman.\nThe war that took place between Parashurama and Bhishmadev was about Kashiraj's three daughters—Ambika, Ambalika, and\nAmba, because Bhishmadev had abducted these three daughters for his brother Vichitravirya. Amba thought that Bhishmadev would marry her, so she became attached to him.\nBut Bhishmadev had taken a vow of celibacy, so he refused to marry. As a result, Amba\nwent to Bhishmapitamah's military guru Parashuram, who told Bhishmadev to marry her.\nTake her. But Bhishmadev refused, so Parashuram fought him so that he would accept marriage.\nAccept. But Parashurama lost, therefore he became pleased with Bhishma. Vicitravirya, named after the one who was killed by Chitrangada. Yasyam Parasharatasakshadvatirnohare kalaa. 21. Vedagupta Muni Krishna yato'hamadhyagam. Having spoken thus to his disciples, the Lord, the teacher of the world, 22. I am the greatest secret for the son, for peace, in this world.\nVichitravirya was forcibly taken by the son of King Kashi. 23 ज़a. The self-controlled one, the one who is praised by the gods, Ambika, and the unmarried one. He who is attached to them, his heart is filled with disease, and he dies. 24). Meaning of the word:\n\nVicitraveerya: Shantanu's son, Vicitraveerya; Cha: Thus; Avarajah: younger brother; Namna: named; Chitrangada: by the Gandharva named Chitrangada;\nHatu: killed; Yasyam: before Shantanu's marriage, in Satyavati's womb; Parasharat: from the semen of Parashara Muni; Sakshat: directly;\nAvatirnah: taking incarnation; Hare: of the Lord; Kala: part; Vedaguptah: protector of the Vedas; Muni: sage; Krishna: Krishna Dvaipayana;"
